WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.737
- Thanks to Audible for
keeping LegalEagle in the air.

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:06.970
(patriotic music)

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.290
Hey, LegalEagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.500
and today we are going to
have a somewhat unusual video

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.070
of some of my favorite YouTubers
who are here at VidCon.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.450
So I thought I would take the opportunity

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.770
to corral some of these great
YouTubers to spend some time

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.120
and talk about some of the legal issues

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.890
that they deal with on an everyday basis

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.340
being creators on YouTube.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.040
And I am thrilled that we have the creator

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.560
of one of my favorite
music-related channels.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.970
This is Cory Arnold from 12tone.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.510
Cory, thank you so much for joining us

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.920
on the yet-to-be-titled,
LegalEagle interview show.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.753
- Yeah, thanks for having me.

00:00:42.753 --> 00:00:44.540
Like Devin said, my name is Cory.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.320
I run a channel called 12tone.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.290
I'm a music theorist and I do a bunch

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.580
of different types of musical analysis.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.830
One of our main series is we'll
look at pop and rock songs

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.130
and the harmony and the
compositional aspects of those

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.820
which I think pretty clearly
falls under commentary

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.460
and critique in terms of fair
use but I still obviously,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.990
I get a lot of fraudulent copyright claims

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:05.990
and I was wondering,

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.840
I know that the actual copyright
claim system inside YouTube

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.700
is not an actual legal entity

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.630
but once it gets to DMCA
takedown notices and stuff,

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.310
I'm wondering what the disincentives are

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:18.720
for major labels and whatever

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.000
to issue those sorts of fraudulent claims

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.270
because I mean, I hope there are some.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.570
- Yeah, well, it's a great
question because obviously,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.260
copyright is a huge issue
on YouTube these days,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.420
both some of the people who
are pirating things illegally

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.830
and creators who are doing things

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:37.910
that are perfectly
within their legal rights

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.600
and yet they are still being claimed

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.260
by some of the rights holders.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.260
We've seen a lot of traffic coming

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.120
from some of the major music studios.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.210
I'm sure you and I
could probably name some

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.680
that are particularly known

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.943
for making false claims against creators.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.760
And so an answer to your question,

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.650
yes, there are actual
penalties for companies

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.710
and individuals that make false claims

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.020
and there are a couple of things.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.340
Most of them stem from what's called

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.980
the Digital Millennium
Copyright Act or the DMCA.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.700
And as you alluded to,
one of the mechanisms

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.320
that a rights holder has to
take down what they consider

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.770
to be illegal material is a DMCA takedown

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.510
and that is basically the company saying

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.030
to a platform or a website,

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.060
you are hosting material that is illegal

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.040
and you should take it down.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.550
I'm giving you notice of it.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.140
And really, if the platform or website

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.120
wants to avoid being liable
for copyright infringement,

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.190
they have to take it
down and that's something

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.160
that a lot of creators don't understand

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.090
is that YouTube sometimes
doesn't have discretion

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.580
to take it down.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.460
But that being said, there
are ways of making sure

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:53.020
that when companies do
take creators' videos down,

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.660
there are some penalties to them.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.370
The first is that when
you issue a DMCA takedown,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.760
that's a real legal document.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.290
That is not the YouTube Content ID system.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.740
This is something that's enshrined in law.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.930
And the most important thing is that

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.510
if you issue a DMCA takedown in bad faith

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.550
and it ultimately goes to court,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.390
you can be liable for attorney's fees.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.900
And so, there's a bit of
a fee-shifting provision

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.590
in the DMCA that if you don't issue

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.700
a takedown in good faith,
you might be on the hook

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.260
for not only your own attorney's fees

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.590
but the attorney's fees of the plaintiff.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.920
Companies, even the studios
and labels are very reluctant

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.610
to issue DMCA takedowns
because they can be on the hook

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.780
for copyright misrepresentation
and they can be on the hook

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.110
for the attorney's fees

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.520
if the creator sues
them under Section 512 f

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.820
which is the misrepresentation
part of that statute.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.790
Unfortunately, creators are not likely

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.210
to sue the rights holders for
making these false claims.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.160
I'd love to hear from your perspective

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.010
what has happened to the extent

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.610
that you have had to deal with

00:03:58.610 --> 00:03:59.950
with some of these takedowns.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.440
- Yeah, so I've gotten a fair
amount of copyright claims

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.320
all from one label who I
probably shouldn't name,

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:06.440
I don't know.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.080
But mainly, just a lot
of videos that I've done,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.980
whenever I've done their songs,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.230
the one time what has gone
a little a bit further,

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:19.230
I actually got one actual
takedown notice from the label,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.640
not the major label that
was making the claim,

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.060
it was the rights holders
for the individual, the band.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.200
- Ah, interesting.
- They issued a takedown

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.020
and then I took it to the next level

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.700
on the YouTube appeals process

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.130
which says effectively either
back off or actually sue me,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.970
take me to court and they
weren't gonna do that.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.660
But once I filed that, pretty quickly,

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.530
I get this email from the
lawyers of that band saying,

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.530
hi, we are this band's lawyer
and we got this notification.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.950
We would like to investigate further

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.810
but we can't watch the video
because it's not on YouTube

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.340
and no one in this office has seen it

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.220
so could you send us a
copy so we can figure out

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.100
if this is copyright infringement?

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.600
I was just like, no, I can't do that.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.433
I didn't have it.

00:05:06.433 --> 00:05:08.290
In the first place, it was an old video

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.980
but it was just like in that case,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.630
if your client didn't
check with their lawyers,

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.600
that feels like a pretty
obvious admission of negligence.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.150
- Yeah, that's pretty crazy

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.620
and it's not at all surprising frankly

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.580
because often the people that issue

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:27.890
the first content ID claims

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:29.880
are not connected to the legal departments

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.140
of these same companies and sometimes,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.640
the training isn't very good
and it will be a non-lawyer

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.250
that issues one of these DMCA takedowns.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.590
And after the DMCA takedown is issued,

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.390
the video goes down and
the lawyers can't view it,

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.430
you can't view it which is hysterical

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.210
that you had to deal with the lawyers

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:52.020
even asking to take a look at your video.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.930
And there's a famous Ninth Circuit case

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.033
called Lenz versus UMG.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.240
People may be familiar with UMG.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.350
- I've heard the name, yes.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:06.710
- And it involves a case where
they issued a DMCA takedown

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:10.120
without even really considering
whether fair use applied

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.920
and the Ninth Circuit
said the DMCA requires you

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.350
to form a good faith belief
before you issue the takedown,

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.630
and not forming a belief

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.390
is not the same as a good faith belief

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.610
and so they got severely reprimanded

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.570
for issuing this takedown

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.700
without really considering fair use.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.020
And so now, it is really
the law of the land,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.160
at least in California and
the rest of the Ninth Circuit

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.520
that you have to at least consider

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.520
whether the video is fair use or not

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.330
or you could be liable
for misrepresentation

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:44.490
and in libel for attorney's
fees for the improper takedown.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:45.323
- And that's interesting.

00:06:45.323 --> 00:06:47.410
It's like in cases where you don't

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:48.980
just get an email from their lawyer

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:50.593
saying we didn't look at this,

00:06:51.508 --> 00:06:53.340
'cause in my understanding,
and I might be wrong here,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.010
but my understanding is
that in terms of fair use,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.650
nothing actually is or
isn't fair use technically

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.170
until a judge rules on it

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.480
so what is the process
of proving that you did

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.610
or didn't have a good faith
belief that it wasn't fair use?

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.410
- Yeah, I think those
are two separate things.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.100
I've heard people say

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.110
nothing can be certain
unless it's litigated

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:12.320
and that's technically true

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.550
but that's technically
true about anything,

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.350
not just copyright.
- Yes, that's true.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.180
- You're never certain until a judge

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.740
has stamped the the judgment.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.470
But the underlying issue of
how would you prove that,

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:28.760
well, in an ideal world,
you would file suit

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.240
and then you would be
entitled to discovery

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.690
which we tend to focus on the drama

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.450
of the trials on TV and in the movies

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:40.320
but the vast majority of what
lawyers do day in and day out

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.790
is actually just the process
of getting all the facts

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:45.270
that are relevant.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:46.530
In The U.S. at least,

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.920
we've sort of done away
with trial by surprise.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.230
We want everyone to know all of the facts

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:55.550
before they get to trial
and so a lot of my job

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.850
has been spent writing discovery requests

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:00.780
where I will ask for the
relevant documents from you

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.440
and in court, you are
obligated to turn those over

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.620
and I will often conduct these interviews

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.480
of the witnesses of the other side,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.710
those are called depositions,

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.260
and so if state of mind is relevant,

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.910
then you would be entitled to sue them

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.350
and then find out all the
people who made the decision

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.230
and you'd be able to put them in a chair

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.320
and you would ask them under oath

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.130
what they thought about
this particular song

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:28.180
that was taken down

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.261
or this video.
- Yeah, a video.

00:08:30.261 --> 00:08:32.200
- And you would be entitled
to all the documents,

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.850
maybe all the email traffic

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.833
where they dealt with
this particular video.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.780
It's a cumbersome process but
that's how you deal with it.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.250
You get the discovery in that topic.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:44.860
I really hope that creators

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.870
are less afraid of copyright
issues in the nature.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.910
I think that there's a lot
of great stuff out there

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.020
and there's a lot of bullying going on,

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:56.540
certainly a lot of copyright infringement

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:57.860
on the internet as well

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:02.050
but for all of these
music channels that I see,

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:05.500
your channel Paul Davids, Adam Neely,

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.930
you guys are doing such great
work with music education.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.110
- Yeah, and we all have
music business education too.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.680
We've taken these classes.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.300
Anyone who has a music degree

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.260
knows at least the
rudiments of copyright law

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:19.093
'cause we have to.

00:09:19.093 --> 00:09:19.926
- Right, right.

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:21.840
- So we know what we're
doing for the most part.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:22.673
- That's awesome.

00:09:22.673 --> 00:09:24.350
Well, I hope you keep at it.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:25.390
I love the channel.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.200
I recommend anyone interested
in music theory 12tone,

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.030
the channel that Cory created,

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:34.160
really is the absolute pinnacle

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.453
of musical analysis on YouTube.

00:09:36.453 --> 00:09:38.990
- Thank you.
- So keep at it

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.950
and hopefully the studios don't
give you too hard of a time

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:45.990
with your material.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:46.910
- I'll just keep appealing.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:48.280
- Yeah, sounds good.

